# Hand-written minimal Matter node. Distinct from the Nordic/CHIP Matter stack:
# this is the from-scratch implementation, and it exists because CHIP does not
# fit a 512 KB / 128 KB part.

config TINYMATTER
	bool "Hand-written minimal Matter node"
	help
	  Compile the from-scratch Matter protocol code: TLV codec, message and
	  exchange layers, MRP, and the small cluster surface Apple Home
	  actually exercises.

	  This is NOT a certified Matter stack and does not aim to be. It exists
	  so a DWM3001CDK can receive its credential reader identity from Apple Home
	  over SetAliroReaderConfig, which is the only way this board can be
	  provisioned without cloning a credential from a donor.

	  Off by default; every stage lands behind it before anything depends
	  on it.

config TINYMATTER_MULTI_ADMIN
	bool "Multi-administrator commissioning: a second ecosystem on this lock"
	depends on TINYMATTER
	default n
	help
	  Hold up to five fabrics instead of three, keep access control per
	  fabric rather than per node, and enforce it: without this the node
	  records a controller's ACL and consults it for nothing, which is
	  survivable while Apple Home is the only administrator and is not once
	  a second one exists.

	  This is what lets Home Assistant be added alongside Apple Home, and
	  therefore what lets anything write a binding.

	  Off, the node is the single-administrator server it has always been
	  and the two spare fabric slots and their access-control storage are
	  not compiled in.

config TINYMATTER_CLIENT
	bool "Matter client: start CASE and send commands to another node"
	depends on TINYMATTER
	# Not a taste dependency: a binding is written by an administrator, and
	# Apple Home does not write bindings. Without a second administrator to
	# do it, a client build is configurable by nobody.
	select TINYMATTER_MULTI_ADMIN
	# Finding the bound peer is a DNS-SD service query, and OpenThread's DNS
	# client is not in a default build: MEASURED at 4,052 B of flash and 160 B
	# of RAM on this image, which is why it arrives with the feature that
	# needs it rather than being on for everyone. Conditional, because this
	# module also builds on hosts and ports that have no OpenThread at all.
	#
	# Guarded on OPENTHREAD and NOT on NET_L2_OPENTHREAD, which is the one
	# that looks right and is not: this app drives OpenThread's own API
	# directly and never brings up Zephyr's L2, so that symbol is unset and a
	# select behind it silently does nothing. MEASURED: the resulting image
	# had CONFIG_TINYMATTER_CLIENT=y and
	# `# CONFIG_OPENTHREAD_DNS_CLIENT is not set`, and still linked, because
	# nothing called the resolver yet and LTO dropped it. It would have
	# failed as an undefined otDnsClientResolveService the moment something
	# did.
	select OPENTHREAD_DNS_CLIENT if OPENTHREAD
	default n
	help
	  Compile the CASE INITIATOR: the half of the handshake this node needs
	  before it can send a command to another node on the fabric, rather
	  than only answer one.

	  This is what lets the lock unlock a SECOND lock directly when the UWB
	  gate fires -- the Matter binding pattern, with no hub automation in
	  the path. The node stays a full Door Lock server either way; the
	  client role is additive.

	  Off, not one byte of it is compiled: the sources live in their own
	  file, guarded in the CMakeLists rather than inside the code.

config TINYMATTER_DOOR_LOCK_ALARMS
	bool "Door Lock DoorLockAlarm event"
	depends on TINYMATTER
	default n
	help
	  Compile the DoorLockAlarm event into the Door Lock cluster by
	  defining MATTER_FEATURE_DL_ALARMS=1 for the module and its callers.

	  Turn it on only for a lock with a sensor that can witness an alarm.
	  Off, every source file preprocesses to what it was before alarms
	  existed, so the default image stays byte-identical.

config TINYMATTER_CRYPTO_PSA
	bool "P-256 and AES-ECB on PSA Crypto (backends/psa)"
	depends on TINYMATTER
	default y
	help
	  Provide the five P-256 seams (DAC signing, operational key
	  generation, CASE ECDH, sign and verify) and crypto_aes_ecb_encrypt()
	  on PSA Crypto. psa_crypto_init() must run before the node starts.
	  Turn it off only to supply those six functions from elsewhere.

choice TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P
	prompt "SPAKE2+ P-256 backend"
	depends on TINYMATTER
	default TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_OBERON if NRF_SECURITY
	default TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_MBEDTLS

config TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_OBERON
	bool "nrf_oberon (nRF Connect SDK, Nordic parts only)"
	help
	  Link the four ocrypto_spake2p_p256_* primitives from nrf_oberon,
	  which nrf_security already links when its PSA driver is the oberon
	  one. No code; see backends/oberon/oberon.cmake.

config TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_MBEDTLS
	bool "Mbed TLS 3.6 ECP (backends/mbedtls)"
	help
	  The same four primitives on Mbed TLS ECP, for any part. Needs
	  MBEDTLS_ECP_C, MBEDTLS_ECP_DP_SECP256R1_ENABLED and
	  MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_C in the Mbed TLS configuration.

endchoice
